      "outcome": "success",
      "prompt": "Goal: CI-OPT slice 2, analysis only: a measured proposal (docs/notes/ci-opt-proposal.md) that separates one-time proofs from continuous checks, locates where the slow grader tests spend their time, and names redundant coverage, for the Test Architect to review before any test moves or is deleted.\nMeasured (Leader, 2026-09-27; profiles in C:/Projects/ci-opt-profile/default.xml and slow.xml, JUnit per-test times on 7be0860): default ring 1792 tests, 1215 s summed, of which the 75 tests of 5 s or more take 763 s (63%), nearly all grader tests (test_grade_architecture, _drift, _mutation, _rigor, _correctness). CI-OPT slice 1 (merged) measured that building the D1 fixture costs about 1.5 s per call, so the other 7-9 s per test is the grader's own work (grading_copy, change_set digests, git archive of the pre-turn tree, or other). Slow ring: 22 tests, 5674 s (94.6 min); three tests take 89%: test_grade_mutation::test_row15_d1_cells_graded_twice_give_characterization_values_and_leave_archives_unchanged (3065 s), test_grade_correctness::test_the_moved_grader_gives_the_gate_runs_0_3_correctness_and_leaves_the_archive_unchanged (1789 s), test_grade_correctness::test_d1_base_with_one_public_assertion_inverted_has_exactly_1_regression (421 s); plus test_grade_rigor::test_the_d1_gate_cells_static_analysis_delta_and_archive_unchanged (202 s). tools/check_regrade.py (the byte-identity gate) grades each gate run twice at each catalog freeze and compares exports, the 0.3 baseline, expected counts and archive integrity. CI (windows-latest, a 2x billing multiplier) runs the default ring in 24-32 min.\nDone when: docs/notes/ci-opt-proposal.md (frontmatter in the shape of docs/notes/*.md; type decision-note; status proposed) holds: (1) where the time goes inside the slow default-ring grader tests: cProfile (python -m cProfile -o, then pstats sorted by cumulative time) of three representative tests, one each from test_grade_architecture, test_grade_drift and test_grade_mutation, with the top 10 cumulative functions per test and the share of the test's time, and one named, concrete way to cut the largest share without changing what the test proves (e.g. a shared pre-turn tree, a cached change_set, a smaller fixture tree), each labelled Verified (measured) or Inferred; (2) a table of every slow-ring test and every default-ring test of 5 s or more (from the two XML files), each classified continuous (it can catch a regression introduced by a normal code change) or one-time (its inputs are frozen: a gate run, a characterization of a frozen archive, a determinism twice-graded check) with the reason, and for each one-time test whether tools/check_regrade.py already proves the same property (name the check_regrade criterion) or not; (3) redundancy: for the mutation files in tests/mutations/*.json, the tests named as killers per mutant; list tests named by no mutant (candidates for \"ceremony\": they may still earn their place, the proposal says what would decide) and groups of tests that are named for exactly the same mutants (candidates for consolidation), as data, not decisions; (4) a ranked list of proposed changes with the measured or estimated minutes saved per default ring, per slow ring and per CI run, and what coverage each change keeps or loses (none may lose a check without saying where that property is still proven); (5) the analysis scripts, if any, live under tools/ci_opt/ with a one-line docstring each, or the note shows the commands used; no test, product code or CI file changes; uv run ruff check src tests tools is clean; Commit the note and scripts.\nNot in scope: moving, deleting or changing any test; ci.yml; any file under runs/ (read only through the existing profiles); running the slow ring again; any push.\nTier: T1\nFan-out cap: 0\nContext ceiling: 150k tokens\nMain-line budget: one slice of at most 50 minutes; commit the note as soon as it is written, then refine.\n\nGrounding: C:/Projects/ci-opt-profile/{default,slow}.xml; docs/coordination/coordination-finish-harness-bench-run.md (the CI-OPT backlog entry and its four goals, including \"a test earns its place by a failure only it catches\"); tools/check_regrade.py (its docstring lists the criteria); tests/mutations/*.json; tests/test_grade_*.py; src/harness_bench/grade/_changes.py; .claude/knowledge/ci-and-test-efficiency.md and .claude/knowledge/testing-strategy.md (read the sections on rings and on what counts as proof). Use python, not python3 (Windows).",
