      "started_at": "2026-09-29T18:14:29Z",
      "summary": "Stopped before authoring: E1 (\"TB2 software-engineering task, easy band\", scenario 5) cannot be built natively (ADR-0013 Amendment 1) from any easy-band Terminal-Bench 2.0 instance.\n\nTB2 established (cited): repo github.com/harbor-framework/terminal-bench-2 (Apache-2.0), pinned commit 2fd12b88aafdd04a52c298e3940bcb189f9766d6 (shallow-cloned and read directly), matching the proposal's own citation (docs/proposals/cross-harness-benchmarking-proposal.md line 57/94/406) and the repo's own README, which states tasks run \"in containerized environments\" and gives `docker_image` as a first-class task.toml field. Task format: one folder per task at repo root, `task.toml` (schema_version, `[metadata].difficulty`, `[environment].docker_image`, `[environment.env]`), `environment/Dockerfile` (+ setup files), `instruction.md` (the agent prompt), `solution/solve.sh` (reference), `tests/test.sh` + `tests/test_outputs.py` (the oracle: test.sh installs uv+pytest and runs test_outputs.py inside the container, writing /logs/verifier/reward.txt). Verified by reading all of these files directly, not from memory.\n\nExhaustive scope: grepped every task.toml in the clone (89 tasks: 4 easy, 55 medium, 30 hard) for `difficulty = \"easy\"`. Exactly 4 easy-band tasks exist: cobol-modernization, fix-git, overfull-hbox, prove-plus-comm. Evaluated all 4, in this order:\n\n1. cobol-modernization — REJECTED. environment/Dockerfile: `FROM python:3.13-slim-bookworm` then `apt-get install -y gnucobol3` to compile/run environment/src/program.cbl, which the hidden test (tests/test_outputs.py) runs and diffs against the agent's Python reimplementation. gnucobol3 is an apt (Debian bookworm) package; no pinned native Windows or macOS GnuCOBOL install was verified, so the toolchain is not provably native+portable without an unverified substitute.\n\n2. fix-git — REJECTED, and not for portability: its toolchain (git + python/pytest) is the most portable of the four, but the task's own mechanic is structurally incompatible with this repo's workspace engine. instruction.md: \"I just made some changes... but now I can't find those changes... find them and merge them into master.\" environment/setup.sh builds the given state by cloning a real repo (github.com/TheMikeMerrill/personal-site, commit d7d3e4b — both verified reachable via `gh api`), resetting to a detached-HEAD commit, amending it, and leaving it reachable only through `.git/logs/HEAD` (the reflog); solution/solve.sh reads the lost commit hash from a fixed reflog line (`sed -n '4p'`) and recovers it. I tested this repo's actual workspace pipeline against that exact shape: `src/harness_bench/workspace.py:task_source()` (`git init` + `git add -A` + `git commit`, tasks/E1 pattern) preserves an existing reflog, but `workspace.py:cell_working_copy()` (`git clone --local` + `git remote remove origin` — the step that builds the actual directory an agent works in) does not: a fresh clone starts a new, empty reflog. I reproduced the whole sequence in a throwaway repo (scratchpad/gitexp): after `task_source`-style init+commit the reflog still showed the original 5 entries including \"Move to Stanford\"; after `cell_working_copy`-style `git clone --local` the clone's reflog showed only `clone: from <path>` — the \"lost\" commit object was still present (`git cat-file -t` returned `commit`, since `--local` hardlinks the object store) but unreachable and invisible to `git reflog`/`git log --all`, i.e. the exact mechanism the task asks the agent to use no longer exists in the cell the agent would receive. This is a verified engine/task incompatibility (src/harness_bench/workspace.py), not a Windows/macOS gap, and fixing it is an engine change, out of scope here (\"Not in scope: any engine, grader or report code\").\n\n3. overfull-hbox — REJECTED. environment/Dockerfile: `FROM ubuntu:24.04` then `apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends texlive-latex-base=2023.20240207-1`, an exact Ubuntu-24.04-apt-pinned TeX Live build; the oracle (tests/test_outputs.py) compiles LaTeX and checks for overfull-hbox warnings. No equivalent pinned-version native TeX Live install for Windows/macOS was verified, so the same build cannot be proven identical cross-platform.\n\n4. prove-plus-comm — REJECTED. environment/Dockerfile: `FROM ubuntu:24.04` then `apt-get install -y coq` (unpinned apt version) to check the completed Coq proof (environment/partial_proof.v) via `coqc`/`coq_makefile`. No pinned native Windows/macOS Coq toolchain was verified as equivalent to the Ubuntu-24.04 apt build the oracle actually runs.\n\nPer the brief's stop condition and the Not-in-scope clause (\"If the inventory row cannot be met as written ... stop and report it rather than redefining the task\"), tasks/E1/task.yaml is left unchanged (status: stub) and bench/bom.yaml is left unchanged. No workspace/, tests/, oracle/ or prompt.md was written for E1; no task was invented and no upstream test was stripped. This commit records the investigation as an audit-log entry only (outcome: blocked).\n\nassume: none of the four rejections rest on an unverified belief — each cites the exact Dockerfile line or a reproduced git experiment. The one open assume is definitional: this run treated \"native on both Windows and macOS\" as requiring a *provably pinned, verified-equivalent* toolchain install on each OS, matching the discipline D2/E6 use for their own ADR-0013 Amendment 1 macOS notes (a named confirming action, not an executed macOS run — no macOS host in this worktree). Confirm: an Owner ruling on whether an unpinned but ubiquitous tool (e.g. a Coq or TeX Live install with no apt pin) would satisfy \"native\" for BOM v0. Breaks if false: 2 (overfull-hbox, prove-plus-comm) of the 4 rejections would need re-review against a looser bar.",
